analysisWe need to read the comment: "// @@@ If the value at the removed index occurred only once, it remains present after removal."

We need to produce an assert statement that captures this specification.

Context: method remove(int index) removes element at index, shifts elements left, returns old value.

Spec: If the value at the removed index occurred only once, it remains present after removal. Wait, that seems contradictory: if it occurred only once, after removal it should no longer be present. The comment says "remains present after removal". Perhaps they mean "does not remain present". But we follow the comment exactly: "If the value at the removed index occurred only once, it remains present after removal." That is illogical but we must produce assert following that.

Interpretation: At pre-state, we have some element v = this.get(index). If count of v in list before removal is exactly 1, then after removal, the list still contains v. That seems impossible, but we encode.

Need to use observer methods: contains(Object o), indexOf(Object o), maybe size, get.

We need to refer to old count. There's no count method; we can express count by quantifying over indices: number of occurrences equals 1. So we need to assert: if (\forall int i; 0 <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(index))) => i == index) i.e., only at index. Or using \old(this.contains(v)) etc. Simpler: we can express that the element at removed index occurs only once before removal: \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (i != index) => !\old(this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(index)))) . That's "only at index".

Then postcondition: this.contains(v) (or indexOf(v) != -1). So final assert: ( \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < \old(this.size()) && i != index; !\old(this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(index)))) ) => this.contains(\old(this.get(index)));

But need to reference the removed index variable "index". In post-state, index variable still same value (method param). So we can use index.

We need to use .equals for object equality. In spec examples they used .equals for query results. Use this.get(i).equals(...). For contains, it uses Objects.equals internally, but we can just use this.contains(v).

Thus final assert:

assert ( \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < \old(this.size()) && i != index; !\old(this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(index)))) ) => this.contains(\old(this.get(index)));

Make sure parentheses correct. Also need to ensure precedence: => lower than &&, so parentheses around quantified spec maybe not needed but fine.

Thus output code block with assert statement.

