analysisWe need to write assert statement for comment: "If the value at the removed index occurred only once, it remains present after removal."

Interpretation: After removing element at index, if that element (value) occurred only once in the list before removal, then after removal it should NOT be present? Wait "remains present after removal" means it remains present, but if it occurred only once before removal, after removal it would be gone. The comment seems contradictory. Let's parse: "If the value at the removed index occurred only once, it remains present after removal." That would be false logically. Maybe they meant "does not remain". But we must follow comment literally.

Thus specification: If the element at that index was unique (occurs exactly once in the list before removal), then after removal, the list still contains that value. That is contradictory but we just encode.

We need to use observers: contains(Object o), indexOf, size, get, maybe count occurrences. There's no direct count method. We can express "only once" using indexOf and lastIndexOf: if indexOf(value) == lastIndexOf(value). That indicates at most one occurrence. Also need to ensure that value is present originally: indexOf(value) != -1. So "occurred only once" means indexOf(value) == lastIndexOf(value) && indexOf(value) != -1. Or equivalently, this.indexOf(value) == this.lastIndexOf(value). Since indexOf returns -1 if not present, then equality holds also when -1 == -1 (not present). So we need also ensure it's present: indexOf(value) != -1.

We need the value at removed index before removal: we can capture using \old(this.get(index)). Let's denote oldVal = \old(this.get(index)). The condition: if oldVal occurs only once before removal, then after removal, the list still contains oldVal. So assert: (\old(this.indexOf(\old(this.get(index)))) != -1 && \old(this.indexOf(\old(this.get(index)))) == \old(this.lastIndexOf(\old(this.get(index))))) => this.contains(\old(this.get(index)));

But need to refer to old occurrences count using old on indexOf and lastIndexOf. Use \old(this.indexOf(...)). So final expression:

assert ( \old(this.indexOf(\old(this.get(index)))) != -1 && \old(this.indexOf(\old(this.get(index)))) == \old(this.lastIndexOf(\old(this.get(index)))) ) => this.contains(\old(this.get(index)));

Simplify: let v = \old(this.get(index)). Then condition: \old(this.indexOf(v)) != -1 && \old(this.indexOf(v)) == \old(this.lastIndexOf(v)). Then guarantee: this.contains(v).

Thus final assert statement.

Make sure syntax: parentheses around implication. Use =>.

Thus output code block with assert statement.

